			<abstract>
				<p>We study the security of CTR-DRBG, one of NIST&apos;s recommended Pseudorandom Number Generator (PRNG) designs. Recently, Woodage and Shumow (Eurocrypt&apos; 19), and then Cohney et al. (S&amp;P&apos; 20) point out some potential vulnerabilities in both NIST specification and common implementations of CTR-DRBG. While these researchers do suggest countermeasures , the security of the patched CTR-DRBG is still questionable. Our work fills this gap, proving that CTR-DRBG satisfies the robustness notion of Dodis et al. (CCS&apos;13), the standard security goal for PRNGs.</p>
			</abstract>
